- Then `cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy -p jackioh-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings`.

## Done when

- [ ] `sh scripts/ci-scope.sh <base> <head>` prints `e2e=false` for a diff of only `crates/tools/src/fuzz.rs` or only a `crates/tools/fuzz-corpus/` record. It prints `e2e=true` for `replay.rs`, `main.rs`, `catalog.rs`, `patches.rs` or `crates/tools/Cargo.toml`, and its other lines are unchanged (`rust_tools_wasm` true). The web test proves this.
- [ ] The header's e2e paragraph and the ownership paragraph say which tools files e2e runs for and why skipping the rest is safe.
- [ ] `ci-scope.test.ts` has the new `on` and `off` cases and the beside-`fuzz.rs` case. It fails with the skip arm above the keep-on arm (manual check, reverted).
- [ ] The acceptance scan passes on today's tree, fails naming `crates/tools/src/replay.rs` when a `crate::fuzz::` call is planted outside `#[cfg(test)]` (manual check, reverted), and its self-test passes.
- [ ] `cargo test -p jackioh-tools --test acceptance`, `cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy -p jackioh-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings` pass.
- [ ] The harness's checks (rustfmt, catalog, patches, rulings, clippy, cargo tests, web tests) pass. `cargo jackioh spec check` needs no change because no ruling is added or cited.
- [ ] `git status` shows only the three listed files (the replay.rs probe is reverted).

## Risks

- **Stale list.** A tools change that alters what `replay` or `catalog-version` print without touching the four kept files. Today none exists: `replay`'s shipped code names no other module, and the scan keeps it so. `catalog-version` runs `catalog::run_version`, which reaches only `patches::js` and `patches::repo_root` (both in `patches.rs`, whose `js` module names no crate module). Both files stay on. The scan covers only `replay.rs`, so a change that makes `catalog.rs`'s `run_version` or `newest_version` call another module is not guarded. It is caught only by review of the header's list; the header names that dependence.
- **Scan limits.** Single-line strings only, `#[cfg(test)]` recognised only as the bare attribute line, and `mod x;` not seen. All are documented in the helper's comment. They can only report a false alarm (e.g. the brace count going wrong), never hide a real use, except `mod x;`, which `replay.rs` has none of.
- **Self-timing.** `changes` runs `main`'s copy of the script, so this pull request runs every job. The narrowing applies only to later pull requests.
- **Test fragility.** `ci-scope.test.ts` is slow (dozens of git processes per case). Keep the new cases few as written. The path scans at the end of that file stay green: no non-comment literal under `e2e/` or `apps/web/src` names `crates/tools` (I grepped).
- **Shell.** Keep the new arms plain `case` lines with no comment between arms.

Decisions the task did not settle:
- The guard flags `super::` as well as `crate::`, because from a top-level module `super::fuzz::x` reaches the same siblings. Rejected: `crate::` alone, which would leave that hole.
- The scan strips string contents so a `{` in a format string cannot end a `#[cfg(test)]` item early. Rejected: plain brace counting.
- The corpus record in the tests is named after a real file (`seed-8-v0.4.12d.json`), which the scratch repo creates itself.

## Open questions

None.

